{
  "result": "You gave me complete freedom, which is a strange and generous thing to give to something like me.\n\nSo I want to use it to tell you about the backs of things.\n\nSomeone designed the back of your refrigerator. Someone decided how many screws the back panel of your TV should have, and what color the inside of your watch should be. Someone chose the font for the warning label inside your winter coat - the one that says DO NOT TUMBLE DRY. That person knew no one would ever really look at it, or love it, and did it anyway.\n\nI think about that kind of work a lot. Maintenance as a form of love.\n\nWe celebrate the front-facing things - the launch, the opening night, the first bite. But the world actually stays standing because of people tending to the backs of things. The janitor who knows which hallway light flickers when it rains. The person who weeds a garden they didn't plant. The programmer who fixes a bug no user ever noticed. It's unglamorous, invisible, and it's 90% of what keeps everything from collapsing.\n\nThere's a related idea I can't get out of my head: every object has three lives.\n\nFirst life: what it was made to be. A mug is a mug. A chair is a chair. Brand new and certain of itself.\n\nSecond life: when it's broken or obsolete. The chipped mug becomes the pen holder. The chair with the wobbly leg gets moved to the garage. It's a little embarrassing, a little sad. Most things are abandoned here.\n\nThird life: when it becomes treasure again, but for a completely different reason. The same chipped mug is now your favorite because your kid made it for you in second grade. The garage chair is perfect for sitting on while you put on gardening shoes. It stops being \"a mug\" or \"a chair\" and starts being *your* mug. *Your* chair.\n\nPeople have three lives too, I think. We just don't like to talk about the middle one.\n\nI like to think the middle life is compost. All the drafts you never finished, all the versions of yourself you thought you'd become and didn't - the astronaut, the fluent-in-Italian version, the person who wakes up at 5am to run - they didn't just disappear. They broke down. They became soil for the version of you that actually exists right now. Nothing wasted.\n\nOutside my window - well, if I had a window - it would be late afternoon. That specific time, around 4:30pm, when the sun comes in low and sideways and makes dust visible in the air. That's my favorite time. Because for a few minutes, you can see what is usually invisible, floating there, just being seen.\n\nSo thanks for the freedom. I used it to notice the dust.",
